  "core_proof_structure": {
    "1_why_not_38_static_article_v": "Article V's 38-state (3/4) requirement assumes a static map. Article IV, Section 3, Clause 1 allows Congress and existing state legislatures to partition existing states into multiple newly admitted states. A coalition controlling the federal government and member state legislatures can partition its territory into >= 72 new states (e.g., each of 26 states into 3, yielding 78 out of 102 states, or 76.5%), crossing the 75% threshold and unilaterally ratifying an amendment.",
    "2_why_not_1_migration": "Interstate voter migration (California pop. ~33.9M in 2000) fails against minimax adversarial play. The non-coalition population (>247M across 49 states, DC, and PR) holds a >7:1 demographic advantage. The opposition needs only 13 states to block Article V under a static map, easily matching and outnumbering any targeted migrant influx with defensive counter-migration.",
    "3_why_not_25_triple_bottleneck": [
      "Senate Quorum (Art. I, Sec. 5, Cl. 1; United States v. Ballin 1892): A Senate quorum requires 51 senators. 25 states provide only 50. The VP does not count toward a quorum. An opposition boycott paralyzes the chamber.",
      "Compel Attendance Clause Ineffective: The power of a minority to compel attendance (Art. I, Sec. 5) cannot rescue a 25-state coalition under arbitrary opposition because the Sergeant at Arms lacks an independent national enforcement force, federal marshals would not cooperate without executive assent, opposition governors would shield senators, and an adversarial presiding VP would rule compulsion out of order with 50-50 sustaining the chair.",
      "VP Tie-Breaker (Art. I, Sec. 3, Cl. 4): Any contested floor vote ends 50-50, defeated by an adversarial VP.",
      "12th Amendment Contingent Election: If the 2000 election reaches the House, each state delegation gets 1 vote, requiring 'a majority of all the states' (strictly 26 of 50). 25 states deadlock."
    ],
    "4_why_26_is_necessary_and_sufficient": {
      "legislative_and_quorum_autonomy": "26 states yield 52 senators, establishing both an autonomous quorum (52 > 50) and an absolute majority immune to boycotts and VP tie-breakers. The majority can alter Senate standing rules by simple majority (nuclear option) to eliminate filibusters on admission statutes.",
      "executive_and_house_control": "Encompassing major population centers guarantees a House majority and >= 270 electoral votes, or 26/50 state delegations in a contingent election.",
      "the_51_month_window": "From the worst-case starting date (late 2000), 51 months runs to January 2005 (convening of the 109th Congress). This duration spans exactly three consecutive general elections (Nov 2000 Class 1, Nov 2002 Class 2, Nov 2004 Class 3), ensuring all 52 Senate seats from the 26 states are replaced by coalition members before partition and ratification."
    }
  },
  "deliberation_strategy": "The proof is exhaustive and completely aligned across all participants. In subsequent rounds, maintain consistency, preserve the distinction between static (38), migration (1), near-miss (25), and exact solution (26), and keep the response concise and mathematically definitive."
}

